---

<DocsLayout
  title="CAM and G-code"
  subtitle="Operations emit a machine-neutral toolpath. A post turns that toolpath into dialect text. Nothing in between writes G-code words."
  description="How CAM works: toolpath IR, operations, posts, the manufacturing kernel, and the dexel part model."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="pipeline" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Pipeline</h2>
    <p>
      <code>packages/core/src/cam/index.ts</code> states the order: drawing entities, then an
      operation, then the toolpath intermediate representation, then a post, then G-code text.
      An operation does not know Fanuc from GRBL. A post does not recompute a pocket. The public
      surface is the <code>cam</code> namespace, plus nested namespaces
      <code>testing</code>, <code>generator</code>, <code>cycles</code>, <code>viewer</code>,
      <code>verify</code>, <code>model</code>, and <code>kernel</code>.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="From a program to text">
{`import { cam } from "@cadcamfun/core"

const post = cam.getPost("grbl")
const text = post && cam.emitProgram(program, post)`}
    </DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="ir" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Toolpath IR</h2>
    <p>
      <code>ir.ts</code> is the contract shared by operations, posts, the 3D playback, and the
      simulator. Coordinates are millimetres, feeds are millimetres per minute, times are
      seconds. Cut depth is negative Z, with the stock top at zero. The operation applies that
      sign once. A post never flips it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Moves are tagged. <code>Rapid</code> and <code>Linear</code> go to a point, and a linear
      move carries a feed. <code>Arc</code> stores the true centre in absolute coordinates. The
      post turns that centre into the relative I and J the dialect wants. An arc whose end has
      the same XY as its start is a full circle. <code>Dwell</code> waits a number of seconds.
      <code>Comment</code> is text. <code>Orient</code> indexes a rotary axis between cuts.
      <code>Cycle</code> is drill, peck, or dwell intent, expanded before some posts emit it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A move may also carry A, B, and C angles that change during the move. That is simultaneous
      motion, distinct from <code>Orient</code>, which happens between moves. Indexed 3+2 work
      is <code>indexing.ts</code>: it tilts the work plane onto a trunnion (AC or BC) and
      transforms the toolpath so ordinary G17 arcs stay valid in machine coordinates.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>tool.ts</code> is the cutter: diameter, flute length, taper, and the rule that only
      a drill plunges straight down. <code>params.ts</code> is the shared height stack
      (clearance, safe, retract), a positive total depth and stepdown, feeds, and coolant. The
      heights are checked in order. <code>path.ts</code> is the planar path (lines and arcs)
      kept separate from Z so offsets, ramps, helices, and tabs share one representation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="operations" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Operations</h2>
    <p>
      Each file in <code>cam/operations</code> is a parameter schema plus a pure
      <code>generate</code> function. <code>common.ts</code> builds the moves. Rapids are not
      emitted below the stock top. Descent is a ramp or a helix limited by an angle. A closed
      profile steps down along the path instead of rapiding at depth. An open profile lifts,
      repositions, and re-enters.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Operation</th><th>What it cuts</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>generateProfile</code></td><td>A contour at a constant offset, in depth passes, with a lead-in and optional tabs. Closed contours: outside, inside, or on the line. Climb milling assumes a clockwise spindle, so an outside contour is travelled counter-clockwise.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>generatePocket</code></td><td>The interior of a closed contour, offset inward until the tool no longer fits.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>generateDrill</code></td><td>Points: a drill cycle or an expanded peck, at the tool diameter.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>generateFace</code></td><td>A raster across the stock top to clean a face.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>generateEngrave</code></td><td>A path followed on the surface at a shallow depth, including text outlines.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>generateSurface</code></td><td>A 3D finish over a height field (raster or similar), not a 2D offset.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>rest</code></td><td>Material the previous cutter could not reach, from a distance field.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>generateThread</code></td><td>A helical thread path from thread parameters.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>generateChamfer</code></td><td>An edge break along a contour with a chamfer tool.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>generateAdaptive</code></td><td>A constant-engagement roughing path.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>generateMultiaxis</code></td><td>Moves that carry rotary angles with the tip motion.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      <code>geometry.ts</code> normalises whatever the caller passed (entities, contours, point
      rings) into contours, and keeps circular sources so a circle can stay an arc in the IR.
      Polylines become arcs only when arc fitting is asked for.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="posts" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Posts</h2>
    <p>
      One emitter, <code>emitProgram</code> in <code>post/emitter.ts</code>, walks the IR and
      tracks modal state (motion mode, feed, plane, tool). A dialect is a small table of how
      that emitter should spell words. Registered dialects are <code>generic</code>,
      <code>fanuc</code>, <code>grbl</code>, and <code>haas</code>. <code>getPost</code> looks
      them up. <code>linuxcnc</code> is a reserved id and is not implemented.
    </p>
    <p>
      Post options set the decimal count, whether canned cycles are emitted or expanded, arc
      mode, tool numbers, and subprograms. <code>formatNumber</code> uses a fixed number of
      decimals. Numbers are not produced with default string conversion.
      <code>post/advanced</code> is a second pass over text that already exists: Fanuc cleanup
      and a Heidenhain conversion. That pass returns an invalid result instead of throwing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="kernel" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Kernel</h2>
    <p>
      <code>cam.kernel</code> is the job that sits above a single operation.
      <code>planJob</code> reads features (from the parametric history, or from hole recognition
      on an exact solid) and returns an ordered list: face, rough, drill or thread, finish,
      deburr, and 3D surface when the part is not 2.5D. The plan assigns cutters. It does not
      cut yet.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>prepareJob</code> fills speeds and feeds from the material and the cutter.
      <code>executeJob</code> runs each operation against a provisional stock, posts the whole
      program once, then replays the G-code independently onto a voxel stock so the evidence is
      not the same code that generated the path. <code>runJob</code> is prepare plus execute.
      The result carries claims and a release level. The functions are pure.
    </p>
    <p>
      Around that pipeline: <code>kinematics.ts</code> solves a trunnion table (forward, inverse
      candidates, unwrapped C, cheapest travel). <code>head.ts</code> is a tilting head about Y.
      <code>collision.ts</code> checks a joint move of the tool against the table and the part.
      <code>motion.ts</code> splits simultaneous five-axis motion into checked blocks.
      <code>stock.ts</code> is a voxel stock: sure removal, possible removal, and a remaining
      volume bracket. <code>machine-clearance.ts</code> checks holder and shank against the table
      on every NC move. <code>convex.ts</code> is the exact distance between convex hulls.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="model" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Part model</h2>
    <p>
      <code>cam/model</code> builds the solid the 3-axis strategies see.
      <code>element-mesh.ts</code> makes a closed mesh per scene element, matching the editor’s
      axis conventions. Parametric histories are united. Booleans go through mesh CSG. A 2D
      entity or text does not become a mesh.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>dexel.ts</code> turns those meshes into columns of Z intervals. Boolean elements
      record which operands they consumed. Strategies read the top height of each column.
      <code>generator/index.ts</code> is the older panel entry, <code>generateToolpath</code>,
      which still drives mill, lathe, and printer paths and then runs the same post. The kernel
      is the path that also plans, verifies, and records evidence.
      <code>brep-part.ts</code> is the fallback when there is no feature history: it recognises
      holes and slabs on the exact solid, or it says the job has to be a full 3D surface.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>verify</code> interprets G-code back into motion and compares it with a height field.
      <code>viewer</code> turns words and lines into a toolpath the 3D view can play.
      <code>cycles</code> is the canned-cycle templates the CAM screen offers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="modules" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Modules</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Module</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>ir.ts</code></td><td>Tagged moves, programs, time estimate, cycle expansion. Millimetres, negative Z.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>operations/</code></td><td>Pure generators from parameters and geometry to IR.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>post/</code></td><td>Shared emitter and the generic, Fanuc, GRBL, and Haas dialects.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>kernel/</code></td><td>Plan, execute, kinematics, collision, voxel stock, clearance.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>setup.ts</code></td><td>Work frame, stock, fixtures, and tool assemblies. See Setups.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>model/</code></td><td>Element meshes and the dexel grid.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>generator/</code></td><td>Panel-facing <code>generateToolpath</code> and suggestions.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>verify/</code>, <code>viewer/</code></td><td>Replay of posted code, and playback geometry.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
